[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Timeline, Cinemachine, and Virtual Cameras in Unity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Creating cinematic sequences in Unity has never been easier thanks to the powerful combination of Timeline and Cinemachine. Timeline allows you to orchestrate cutscenes, gameplay events, and animations on a multi-track editor, while Cinemachine provides a suite of smart camera behaviors that dynamically track targets, blend smoothly, and react to your scene without writing complex camera math."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Setup"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before starting, ensure you have installed the necessary packages through the Unity Package Manager. You will need both the Timeline package and the Cinemachine package. Once installed, your project is ready to orchestrate advanced camera sequences."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Open the Package Manager via Window > Package Manager."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Search for and install 'Cinemachine' and 'Timeline'."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Setting up Cinemachine Virtual Cameras"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cinemachine Virtual Cameras (vcam) act as directors for your main Unity Camera. They do not render the scene directly; instead, they relay their position, rotation, and lens settings to the Main Camera, which holds the CinemachineBrain component."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Go to the top menu and select Cinemachine > Create Virtual Camera."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Select your Main Camera and verify that the 'CinemachineBrain' component is attached. If not, add it manually."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Adjust the Virtual Camera properties, setting your player or target transform in the 'Follow' and 'Look At' fields."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Creating a Timeline Sequence"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Timeline allows you to sequence animations, audio, and crucially, Cinemachine camera switches over time. To begin, create a Timeline asset in your project."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Open the Timeline window via Window > Sequencing > Timeline."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create an empty GameObject in your Hierarchy, select it, and click 'Create' in the Timeline window to generate a new Timeline asset and Director component."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Integrating Cinemachine with Timeline"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"To control your Virtual Cameras via Timeline, you need to add a Cinemachine Track. This track type lets you blend seamlessly between multiple Virtual Cameras by simply dragging them onto the timeline."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Right-click inside the Timeline track area and select 'Cinemachine Shot Track'."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Drag your Virtual Camera from the Hierarchy and drop it onto the newly created Cinemachine Shot Track to create a clip."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Extend or shorten the clip to determine how long that specific camera view remains active."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 4: Triggering the Timeline via Script"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Often, you will want to trigger your cutscene or camera sequence via gameplay events, such as a player entering a trigger zone. You can control the PlayableDirector component programmatically using C#."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.Playables;

public class CutsceneTrigger : MonoBehaviour
{
    public PlayableDirector timelineDirector;

    private void OnTriggerEnter(Collider other)
    {
        if (other.CompareTag("Player"))
        {
            if (timelineDirector != null)
            {
                timelineDirector.Play();
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining Unity's Timeline with Cinemachine Virtual Cameras, developers can build complex, professional-grade cinematics and dynamic gameplay camera switches without writing tedious custom camera animation scripts. Experiment with different Cinemachine body and aim properties to refine the look and feel of your camera transitions."}]
